            <ArticleTitle>Angina pectoris in a child with sickle cell anemia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>911-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 7-year-old black boy with sickle cell disease, Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome, mild left ventricular dysfunction, and normal coronary arteries developed angina pectoris five months after cessation of hypertransfusion therapy. Exercise-induced ECG ST segment depression associated with angina disappeared following transfusion therapy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
